---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ScientificNotation from '@/components/tools/calc2/ScientificNotation.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="scientific-notation-converter">
  <ScientificNotation client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the scientific notation converter works</h2>
    <p>
      The scientific notation converter moves the decimal point until one non-zero digit is left in front
      of it. The number of places moved is the power of ten: moves to the left give a positive power,
      moves to the right a negative one.
    </p>
    <pre><code>scientific    a × 10ⁿ, 1 ≤ |a| &lt; 10
E notation    aEn (6.022E23 means 6.022 × 10²³)
engineering   a × 10ⁿ, n a multiple of 3, 1 ≤ |a| &lt; 1000</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked examples</h2>
    <p>
      0.00004056: the point moves 5 places to the right, so it is 4.056 × 10⁻⁵, or 4.056E-5, or
      40.56 × 10⁻⁶ in engineering notation (40.56 micro). 299,792,458 moves 8 places to the left:
      2.99792458 × 10⁸.
    </p>

    <h2>Exact digits</h2>
    <p>
      The converter works on the digits as text, so long numbers keep every digit and 0.1 stays 0.1. The
      trailing zeros you type after a decimal point are kept, because they are significant: 3.00e8 stays
      3.00 × 10⁸. Enter a number of significant figures to round the result.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The animation draws numbers of up to 40 digits. Longer numbers convert, but are shown in notation
      only. Exponents up to ±100,000 are accepted.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
